# Local notes not intended for public documentation
private-notes/
scratch/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Infrastructure as Code
#
# Everything below this line exists because the repository gained OpenTofu,
# Packer and Ansible sources. The rules fall into three groups:
#
#   1. State and cache files that are machine-specific and often contain
#      secrets in plaintext.
#   2. Variable files that the tools load AUTOMATICALLY, which makes an
#      accidental "git add -A" a one-keystroke credential leak.
#   3. Build and run artifacts that are regenerated on demand.
#
# The one file in this area that MUST be committed is called out explicitly
# at the bottom.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- OpenTofu / Terraform state ---
# State holds every attribute the provider read back from Proxmox, including
# cloud-init passwords and the firewall's WAN address. OpenTofu's native state
# encryption protects the file at rest, but an encrypted secret committed to a
# public repository is still a secret waiting for a passphrase leak.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
.terraform/
.terraform.tfstate.lock.info

# --- OpenTofu / Terraform crash dumps ---
# A crash log is a full stack dump of the running process. It can contain
# request bodies, and therefore API tokens.
crash.log
crash.*.log

# --- Auto-loaded variable files ---
# OpenTofu loads *.auto.tfvars and Packer loads *.auto.pkrvars.hcl WITHOUT
# being asked. That convenience is exactly why they must never be tracked.
# Commit the .example files instead; they carry placeholders, not values.
*.auto.tfvars
*.auto.tfvars.json
terraform.tfvars
terraform.tfvars.json

# --- Packer ---
# manifest.json records the VMID and build UUID of the last template build.
# It is a per-host build artifact, and feeding it back into OpenTofu's
# clone{} block would be actively dangerous (clone is ForceNew).
*.auto.pkrvars.hcl
packer/**/manifest.json
manifest.json
packer_cache/
packer/common.pkrvars.hcl

# --- direnv ---
# .envrc holds every credential the stack needs. .envrc.example is the
# committed template.
.envrc
.direnv/

# --- Ansible ---
# A retry file lists the hosts that failed, which leaks internal inventory
# naming into the repository for no benefit.
*.retry
ansible/roles/wazuh-ansible/
ansible/*.log

# --- Generated credentials ---
# The Wazuh all-in-one installer writes its generated admin, indexer and API
# passwords into this tarball. It is the single highest-value file the lab
# produces and it must never be committed.
wazuh-install-files.tar
wazuh-passwords.txt
*-credentials.txt

# --- DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED ---
# .terraform.lock.hcl IS COMMITTED ON PURPOSE. Do not add it above.
#
# The lock file pins the exact provider version and the checksums of every
# platform's provider binary. Committing it is what makes "bpg/proxmox
# = 0.111.1" a real guarantee rather than an intention: without it, a fresh
# `tofu init` on another machine can resolve a different build of the same
# version, and a supply-chain swap of the provider binary would go unnoticed.
# This lab pins that provider exactly because v0.109.0 inverted the semantics
# of an agent setting; the lock file is the mechanism that pin relies on.
#
# If you ever see .terraform.lock.hcl show up as untracked, commit it.
!.terraform.lock.hcl

# Dot-prefixed secret directories.
#
# The `secrets/` pattern above does NOT match `.secrets/` - the leading dot makes
# it a different name. Both the repo root (scripts/rotate-lab-credentials.sh
# writes here) and ansible/ use the dotted form, so it needs its own entry.
.secrets/

# Backups of .envrc.
#
# `.envrc` itself is ignored above, but a backup named `.envrc.bak-<timestamp>`
# is a DIFFERENT filename and was not covered - so `git add -A` swept three of
# them, containing the old password in plaintext, straight into a commit.
# Caught before pushing. Any sibling of .envrc is a credential file too.
.envrc.*
!.envrc.example
